/**
Event Management System – FAQ
If you have any questions or run into issues, this FAQ is here to help.
All users can reference this guide to understand features, troubleshoot problems, and make the most of TaskCLI with GitHub integration.

1.Getting Started
Q: How do I install the Event Management System?  
A: Clone the repository, install dependencies with npm install (or yarn), and configure your .env file with MongoDB, Stripe, and JWT secrets. Then run both the backend (npm start in /server) and frontend (npm start in /client).

Q: Do I need GitHub access to use TaskCLI integration?  
A: Yes. You’ll need a GitHub account and a Personal Access Token (PAT) with repo and workflow scopes to link TaskCLI to GitHub.

Q: Where do I run TaskCLI commands?  
A: TaskCLI runs in your terminal. Navigate to your project directory and use commands like taskcli init or taskcli sync.

2.Features & Functionality
Q: Can I create events with multiple ticket types?  
A: Yes. Event creation supports different ticket categories (e.g., General Admission, VIP) with separate pricing.

Q: Do task status changes sync with GitHub?  
A: Yes. When TaskCLI is correctly linked, status updates (e.g., “In Progress”, “Completed”) sync with the corresponding GitHub issue or pull request.

Q: Can I create a task from a GitHub issue?  
A: Absolutely. Use taskcli create --from-issue < issue-number > to generate a task directly from an existing GitHub issue.

Q: Does the system send email reminders to attendees?  
A: Yes. Automated notifications are sent for registration confirmations, event reminders, and updates.

3.Troubleshooting Common Issues
Q: My MongoDB connection fails. What should I check?  
A: Ensure MongoDB is running locally or verify your cloud connection string in .env. Common mistake: forgetting to whitelist your IP in MongoDB Atlas.

Q: Stripe payments aren’t working.  
A: Double‑check your Stripe API keys. Test mode keys won’t process live payments.

Q: TaskCLI isn’t syncing with GitHub.  
A: Confirm your PAT is valid and stored with taskcli config set github.token. Also check repository permissions.

Q: I’m getting CORS errors.  
A: Update backend CORS settings to allow requests from your frontend domain (e.g., http://localhost:3000).

4.Specific Area of Interest – GitHub Integration with TaskCLI
Q: How do I link TaskCLI to GitHub?  
A: Run:

bash
taskcli config set github.token < your_personal_access_token>
taskcli config set github.repo < owner/repo-name>
